.el .ds Aq '
.TH openfd 1  "openfd 0.1.0" 
.SH NAME
openfd \- lsof that answers instantly: list open file descriptors of every process
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBopenfd\fR [\fB\-p\fR|\fB\-\-pid\fR] [\fB\-c\fR|\fB\-\-command\fR] [\fB\-u\fR|\fB\-\-user\fR] [\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-fd\fR] [\fB\-\-path\fR] [\fB\-\-dir\fR] [\fB\-\-wait\fR] [\fB\-\-interval\fR] [\fB\-i\fR|\fB\-\-inet\fR] [\fB\-d\fR|\fB\-\-deleted\fR] [\fB\-t\fR|\fB\-\-terse\fR] [\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-summary\fR] [\fB\-\-tree\fR] [\fB\-\-top\fR] [\fB\-\-watch\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-numeric\fR] [\fB\-\-sizes\fR] [\fB\-j\fR|\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-csv\fR] [\fB\-\-color\fR] [\fB\-\-completions\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
lsof that answers instantly: list open file descriptors of every process
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-pid\fR \fI<PID[,PID]...>\fR
Comma\-separated pids to include (\-p in lsof)
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-command\fR \fI<NAME[,NAME]...>\fR
Comma\-separated command names (match on comm and cmdline basename)
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-user\fR \fI<USER[,USER]...>\fR
Comma\-separated users (names or uids)
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-fd\fR \fI<FD[,FD]...>\fR
Comma\-separated fd numbers to include
.TP
\fB\-\-path\fR \fI<SUBSTR[,SUBSTR]...>\fR
Match target paths by substring
.TP
\fB\-\-dir\fR \fI<DIR>\fR
Only fds whose target is inside DIR (lsof `+D`, non\-recursive match on the path prefix; the directory itself must exist)
.TP
\fB\-\-wait\fR
Block until no entry matches the filters, then exit 0 (combine with \-\-path/\-\-dir/\-i; poll interval via \-\-interval)
.TP
\fB\-\-interval\fR \fI<SECONDS>\fR [default: 1]
Poll interval in seconds for \-\-wait/\-\-watch/\-\-top
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-inet\fR [\fI<[PROTO][:PORT][@ADDR]>\fR]
Internet/socket filter, lsof\-style: `\-i` (any inet), `\-i tcp`, `\-i :443`, `\-i tcp:443`, `\-i @127.0.0.1:8080`, `\-i unix`
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-deleted\fR
Only files that were deleted while still open (reclaimable disk space)
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-terse\fR
Print pids only
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-summary\fR
Per\-process summary instead of one line per fd
.TP
\fB\-\-tree\fR
Group output as a process tree (table format only)
.TP
\fB\-\-top\fR [\fI<N>\fR]
fd\-leak detector: scan twice (interval apart), print the top N processes by fd growth. [N] defaults to 10
.TP
\fB\-\-watch\fR
Live mode: on every tick print newly opened (+) and closed (\-) fds that match the filters; runs until interrupted
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-numeric\fR
Skip username resolution (numeric uids, faster)
.TP
\fB\-\-sizes\fR
Stat every fd and show file sizes
.TP
\fB\-j\fR, \fB\-\-json\fR
NDJSON output (one object per line, streaming)
.TP
\fB\-\-csv\fR
CSV output
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR \fI<WHEN>\fR [default: auto]
When to color the output: auto (default), always, never
.TP
\fB\-\-completions\fR \fI<SHELL>\fR
Print a shell completion script (bash, zsh, fish, powershell) and exit
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
bash
.IP \(bu 2
elvish
.IP \(bu 2
fish
.IP \(bu 2
powershell
.IP \(bu 2
zsh
.RE
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH EXTRA
MVP covers the common 20% of lsof use cases. Run `openfd \-\-help` for details.
